.TH CERTMONGER 8 "October 26, 2026" "certmonger Manual"

.SH NAME
acme\-submit

.SH SYNOPSIS
acme\-submit \-u DIRECTORY\-URL
[\-R ca\-cert\-file]
[\-k eab\-kid \-K eab\-key\-file]
[\-a tpm]
[\-P permanent\-identifier]
[\-s state\-directory]
[\-T tcti]
[\-\-tsig\-key\-file file | \-\-gss\-tsig [\-\-keytab keytab] [\-\-principal principal]]
[\-\-dns\-update\-server host[:port]]
[\-\-dns\-zone zone]
[\-\-dns\-resolver address[:port]]
[\-\-dns\-check\-servers host[:port],...]
[\-\-dns\-wait seconds]
[\-v]
[csr\-filename]

.SH DESCRIPTION
\fIacme\-submit\fR is the helper which \fIcertmonger\fR can use to
obtain certificates from servers which implement the Automatic Certificate
Management Environment (ACME) protocol, as described in RFC 8555.  It is
not normally run interactively, but it can be for troubleshooting
purposes.

When called by \fIcertmonger\fR, \fIacme\-submit\fR reads the server's
directory, creates an account if it does not already have one, places an
order for the identifiers named in the signing request, answers the
challenges of the order's authorizations, finalizes the order with the
signing request which \fIcertmonger\fR generated, and prints the issued
certificate.  If the server needs more time, \fIacme\-submit\fR tells
\fIcertmonger\fR to check on the order again later, after the delay
suggested by the server's \fIRetry\-After\fR header.

The account key is an ECDSA P\-256 key, which \fIacme\-submit\fR generates
the first time it uses a server, and keeps with the account's URL in a
directory of its own under the state directory.  The account is reused for
every later order, including renewals.

If the server requires an external account binding (RFC 8555, section
7.3.4), the key identifier and the MAC key which the CA issued are taken
from the challenge password given to \fBgetcert request\fR using its
\fB\-L\fR or \fB\-l\fR option, in the form \fIKID\fR:\fIKEY\fR, or else from
the \fB\-k\fR and \fB\-K\fR options.  The MAC key is base64url\-encoded.

.SS Identifiers and challenges
Without device attestation, the order names the DNS names and IP addresses
in the signing request's subjectAltName extension, or, if it has none, its
subject's common name.  Authorizations must then be valid already (as they
are when the server authorizes the account through its external account
binding) or be satisfied by a challenge which \fIacme\-submit\fR can
answer: \fIdns\-01\fR, if it is given a TSIG key or GSS\-TSIG to update the
DNS with.  Challenges which require serving content (\fIhttp\-01\fR,
\fItls\-alpn\-01\fR) are not supported.

With device attestation, the order names the device's
\fIpermanent\-identifier\fR (draft\-ietf\-acme\-device\-attest), which is the
value given with the \fB\-P\fR option, or else the common name of the
signing request's subject, and \fIacme\-submit\fR answers the
\fIdevice\-attest\-01\fR challenge.

.SS DNS challenges
To answer a \fIdns\-01\fR challenge (RFC 8555, section 8.4) for a name,
\fIacme\-submit\fR adds a TXT record holding the base64url\-encoded SHA\-256
digest of the challenge's key authorization at \fI_acme\-challenge\fR.\fIname\fR
(without the \fI*.\fR label of a wildcard name), or at the name which a
CNAME record there points to, using a DNS UPDATE message (RFC 2136) signed
with TSIG (RFC 8945) or GSS\-TSIG (RFC 3645).  The update goes to the
primary server of the record's zone, which is found by looking up SOA
records from the record's name upwards.  \fIacme\-submit\fR then asks each
of the zone's name servers, directly, until they all serve the record,
tells the server that the challenge can be checked, waits for it to be,
and removes the record, whatever the outcome.  No other program is run.

With GSS\-TSIG, Kerberos credentials are obtained using the keytab, in a
credential cache of the helper's own, and a key is set up with the DNS
server, in TKEY queries, for its \fIDNS/\fRserver\fI@REALM\fR principal, as
the DNS servers of FreeIPA and Active Directory expect from the hosts of
their domains.  The server's name must then be known: an address can't be
given with \fB\-\-dns\-update\-server\fR.

.SS TPM attestation
With \fB\-a tpm\fR, the private key must have been generated in a TPM
through the \fItpm2\-pkcs11\fR PKCS#11 module, in a token which is
accessible through an NSS database, using \fBgetcert request \-d
\fIDATABASE\fR \-t \fITOKEN\fR\fR.  \fIacme\-submit\fR finds the key by the
public key in the signing request, and answers the challenge with a WebAuthn
attestation object of the \fItpm\fR format, in which an attestation key
(AK) of the TPM certifies the key using TPM2_Certify.  The digest of the
challenge's key authorization is the qualifying data of the certification.

The AK is a restricted signing key which \fIacme\-submit\fR creates under
the TPM's endorsement key (EK) the first time it is needed, and keeps in the
account's directory.  Its certificate is obtained from the server's
attestation key certification service, next to the ACME directory: the
server encrypts an HMAC key to the EK, which the TPM imports and uses to
certify the AK, proving that the AK and the EK belong to the same TPM.  The
EK's certificate is read from the TPM's non\-volatile memory, along with the
certificates of the CAs which issued it, which the TPM keeps there too, or
else which the certificate names the location of.  The
AK's certificate is renewed when less than 30 days of its validity remain.

The key's PIN is the one given to \fBgetcert request\fR using its \fB\-p\fR
or \fB\-P\fR option, which \fIcertmonger\fR passes to the helper.  The
endorsement hierarchy must not be protected by a password.

.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-u\fR \fIURL\fR, \fB\-\-directory\fR=\fIURL\fR
The location of the server's ACME directory, which must be an \fIhttps\fR
URL.  This option is always required.
.TP
\fB\-R\fR \fIFILE\fR, \fB\-\-cacert\fR=\fIFILE\fR
The location of the certificate(s), in PEM form, of the CA which issued
the server's certificate.  Only these certificates are trusted when
verifying the server's certificate.  If this option is not given, the
system's trusted CA certificates are used.
.TP
\fB\-k\fR \fIKID\fR, \fB\-\-eab\-kid\fR=\fIKID\fR
The key identifier for the external account binding.
.TP
\fB\-K\fR \fIFILE\fR, \fB\-\-eab\-key\-file\fR=\fIFILE\fR
The location of a file containing the MAC key for the external account
binding, base64url\-encoded, used if no challenge password was given for the
request.
.TP
\fB\-a\fR \fIFORMAT\fR, \fB\-\-attest\fR=\fIFORMAT\fR
Request the device's permanent identifier, and answer \fIdevice\-attest\-01\fR
challenges with an attestation of the given format.  The only format which
is supported is \fItpm\fR.
.TP
\fB\-P\fR \fIVALUE\fR, \fB\-\-permanent\-identifier\fR=\fIVALUE\fR
The permanent identifier of the device, as registered with the CA.
.TP
\fB\-s\fR \fIDIRECTORY\fR, \fB\-\-state\-dir\fR=\fIDIRECTORY\fR
The directory under which account and attestation data are kept.  The
default is the value of the \fBCERTMONGER_ACME_DIR\fR environment variable,
or \fI@CM_STORE_ACME_DIRECTORY@\fR.
.TP
\fB\-T\fR \fITCTI\fR, \fB\-\-tcti\fR=\fITCTI\fR
How to reach the TPM, in the form which the TSS's TCTI loader accepts, for
example \fItabrmd\fR or \fIdevice:/dev/tpmrm0\fR.  The default is the value
of the \fBTPM2_PKCS11_TCTI\fR environment variable, which \fItpm2\-pkcs11\fR
also uses, then that of \fBTPM2TOOLS_TCTI\fR, then the TSS's default.
.TP
\fB\-\-tsig\-key\-file\fR=\fIFILE\fR
Answer \fIdns\-01\fR challenges, signing updates with the TSIG key in the
file, which holds a \fIkey\fR statement in the form which BIND's
\fItsig\-keygen\fR writes, or a line in the form
[\fIalgorithm\fR:]\fIname\fR:\fIsecret\fR, which \fInsupdate\fR's \fB\-y\fR
option takes.  The algorithm is \fIhmac\-sha256\fR (the default),
\fIhmac\-sha384\fR, \fIhmac\-sha512\fR or \fIhmac\-sha224\fR.
.TP
\fB\-\-gss\-tsig\fR
Answer \fIdns\-01\fR challenges, signing updates with GSS\-TSIG.
.TP
\fB\-\-keytab\fR=\fIKEYTAB\fR
The keytab to use with GSS\-TSIG.  The default is the system's default
keytab, usually \fI/etc/krb5.keytab\fR.
.TP
\fB\-\-principal\fR=\fIPRINCIPAL\fR
The principal to use with GSS\-TSIG.  The default is the first one in the
keytab.
.TP
\fB\-\-dns\-update\-server\fR=\fIHOST\fR[:\fIPORT\fR]
The server to send updates to.  The default is the zone's primary server,
which its SOA record names.
.TP
\fB\-\-dns\-zone\fR=\fIZONE\fR
The zone to update.  The default is the zone which the record's name is in.
.TP
\fB\-\-dns\-resolver\fR=\fIADDRESS\fR[:\fIPORT\fR]
The DNS resolver to send lookups to.  The default is to use the system's.
.TP
\fB\-\-dns\-check\-servers\fR=\fIHOST\fR[:\fIPORT\fR],...
The servers which must serve the record before the server is told to check
it.  The default is the zone's name servers.
.TP
\fB\-\-dns\-wait\fR=\fISECONDS\fR
How long to wait for those servers to serve the record.  The default is 120
seconds.
.TP
\fB\-v\fR, \fB\-\-verbose\fR
Increases the logging level.  Use twice or more for more logging.  This
option is mainly useful for troubleshooting.  Secrets are never logged.

.SH FILES
.TP
\fI@CM_STORE_ACME_DIRECTORY@/\fBHASH\fP/\fR
The data kept for one ACME directory URL, where \fBHASH\fR is derived from
the URL: \fIaccount.key\fR, \fIaccount.url\fR, and, for TPM attestation,
\fIak.pub\fR, \fIak.priv\fR and \fIak.crt\fR.  The helper holds a lock on
the \fIlock\fR file there while it runs, so that requests which use the
same account are handled one at a time.

.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
0
if the certificate was issued.  The certificate will be printed in
PEM\-encoded form.
.TP
2
if the CA rejected the request, an authorization, the attestation, or the
challenge's TXT record.  An
error message may be printed.
.TP
3
if the CA, or a DNS server, was unreachable, or the CA's certificate could
not be verified, or the zone's servers did not serve the TXT record in
time.  An
error message may be printed.
.TP
4
if critical configuration information is missing, or a DNS server refused
an update or its signature.  An error message may be
printed.
.TP
5
if the CA is still thinking.  A suggested poll delay (specified in seconds)
and a cookie (the order's URL) will be printed.

.SH BUGS
Please file tickets for any that you find at https://codeberg.org/freeipa/certmonger/issues

.SH SEE ALSO
